{
  "requirement": "Each requirement and example declaration badge has two adjacent links. Its\nkind (`req` or `example`) links to the declaration's anchor on the current\npage; its full name links to the evidence detail page. These segments stay\ntogether in one non-wrapping row, with no separate arrow link or plaintext evidence link.\nEach detail page links back to the declaration's anchored badge and to the\nindex. Reference badges continue to link to the declaration. These links\nresolve when a requirement name contains `.md`, as in\n`requirements.cli.mdbook`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every clause of `requirements.index.navigation`.\n\n- **Kind link and name link (req and example).** `declaration_sections_contain_their_headers_and_markdown_bodies` checks the summary header for `req` and `example` declarations. The kind anchor must have `href=\"#habeas-{kind}-rules.{name}\"` and the text `{kind}`. The name anchor must have `href=\"requirements-index/rules.{name}.html\"` and the title \"View evidence for ...\". The anchor `id` is also checked. `declaration_badges_and_margin_controls_share_kind_colors` and `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages` repeat the anchor and evidence-link checks across kinds and nested chapters. For nested chapters they check relative hrefs such as `../requirements-index/rules.child.nested.html`.\n- **One non-wrapping row, two adjacent links.** `mdbook_preprocessor_renders_definitions_between_stdin_and_stdout` (end-to-end) splits the output on `habeas-definition-badge` spans. Each row must contain exactly two `</a>` elements, one `habeas-badge-kind` and one `habeas-badge-name`. `declaration_badge_stylesheet_keeps_split_links_in_one_row` asserts the CSS: `inline-flex`, `flex-wrap: nowrap`, `white-space: nowrap`, and `flex-shrink: 0` on the links.\n- **No separate arrow link or plaintext evidence link.** The header must not contain `habeas-detail-icon`. The content must not contain \"See evidence\" or `<svg`. These checks are in `declaration_sections_contain_their_headers_and_markdown_bodies`, `index_owns_details_in_book_order_with_badge_icons` and `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages`.\n- **Detail pages link back to the anchored badge and to the index.** `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages`, `index_owns_details_in_book_order_with_badge_icons` and `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` assert `[Requirements index](../requirements-index.html)` and `[View definition](...#habeas-req-...)`. They do this for requirements and examples, at several nesting depths, and with a custom index path.\n- **Reference badges still link to the declaration.** `references_in_nested_chapters_link_to_the_requirement_definition` checks that `impl`, `cite` and `test` badges in a nested chapter have `href=\"../requirements.html#habeas-req-requirements.ready\"`.\n- **Links resolve for names containing `.md`.**\n  - `links_preserve_requirement_names_containing_md` tests `chapter_href` for both the detail-page and the definition-anchor directions. Both give `%2Emd`.\n  - The `cli.mdbook` name is also checked end to end. The kind anchor and the name anchor both use `%2Emd`, and so does the evidence link. The detail-page \"View definition\" link uses it for both a requirement and an example.\n  - `chapter_href` is also the helper that builds the detail hrefs in `replace_definitions`, whose anchor permalinks use the same `.md` escaping. No test puts an `.md` name in a reference badge itself. The `chapter_href` unit test covers the shared mechanism, so I don't count this as a gap.\n- **Escaping of hrefs.** `definition_evidence_link_targets_are_escaped` covers hostile index paths. It goes beyond what the requirement asks.\n\nI found no behavior that violates the requirement and would still pass these tests. No acknowledgments are needed.",
  "references": "4e0e60c4f8a54bb02a235630bf39f7a53d1b2376299a4958a791682aff9a7597",
